WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.826
Cryptographic strength depends heavily on the values around the algorithm.

2
00:00:03.826 --> 00:00:12.245
Secret keys, public keys, salts, initialization values, IVs, nonces, and random values all have different purposes, and confusing them can weaken a

3
00:00:12.245 --> 00:00:12.627
design.

4
00:00:12.627 --> 00:00:14.158
Keys are sensitive assets.

5
00:00:14.158 --> 00:00:20.663
They need clear ownership, approved generation, controlled storage, rotation policy, access limits, and deletion or retirement behavior.

6
00:00:20.663 --> 00:00:25.255
A strong algorithm cannot compensate for exposed or poorly managed key material.

7
00:00:25.255 --> 00:00:29.464
Cryptographic randomness should come from approved library or operating-system facilities.

8
00:00:29.464 --> 00:00:36.734
General-purpose pseudo-random functions are not appropriate for key generation, nonces that require unpredictability, or other security-sensitive

9
00:00:36.734 --> 00:00:40.178
values unless an approved cryptographic API is providing them.

10
00:00:40.178 --> 00:00:42.856
Salts, IVs, and nonces are not interchangeable.

11
00:00:42.856 --> 00:00:47.065
A salt supports uniqueness in password hashing or related derivation contexts.

12
00:00:47.065 --> 00:00:50.892
An IV or nonce may support a specific encryption mode.

13
00:00:50.892 --> 00:00:58.545
Some values must be unique, some must be unpredictable, and some can be public but still must follow strict rules.

14
00:00:58.545 --> 00:01:03.136
Nonce reuse can be security-critical depending on the algorithm and mode.

15
00:01:03.136 --> 00:01:08.111
The application should not rely on informal memory of what was used before.

16
00:01:08.111 --> 00:01:13.468
It should have a clear generation and persistence strategy suitable for the library API.

17
00:01:13.468 --> 00:01:16.146
Key lifecycle decisions belong in the design.

18
00:01:16.146 --> 00:01:25.330
Define where keys come from, how they rotate, which components can read them, how old data remains accessible, and how failures are handled during

19
00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:25.712
transition.

20
00:01:25.712 --> 00:01:35.278
The safe habit is to treat randomness and key material as first-class design elements, not incidental parameters passed into a crypto call at the

21
00:01:35.278 --> 00:01:36.044
last moment.

22
00:01:36.044 --> 00:01:39.487
Reviewers should also look for accidental reuse across environments.

23
00:01:39.487 --> 00:01:45.992
Development, test, staging, and production should not share sensitive key material just because it makes deployment easier.

24
00:01:45.992 --> 00:01:52.880
Clear ownership makes recovery safer when a key must be rotated, retired, or replaced after an operational incident.
